WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.636
Hands-on episode.

2
00:00:00.636 --> 00:00:05.128
We add one small new command — get clipboard text — twice: once as a real Tauri command in Rust, once as a browser shim.

3
00:00:05.128 --> 00:00:07.000
Same invoke call site, unmodified, on both builds.

4
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.641
The target: get_clipboard_text.

5
00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:16.000
A tiny, self-contained command — no dependencies on anything else in the app — so the whole shim pattern from E68 is visible without noise.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:16.541
Rust side first.

7
00:00:16.541 --> 00:00:21.512
A #[tauri::command] function, using the arboard crate or Tauri's own clipboard plugin to read the system clipboard and return a String or an error.

8
00:00:21.512 --> 00:00:30.000
Then the one easy step to forget: add it to generate_handler's list in the invoke_handler chain — a command that exists but isn't listed there is invisible to the frontend and fails with a not-found error that looks nothing like a permissions problem.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.740
Browser side: a new tiny file, web/shims/clipboard.

10
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.888
ts, importing registerCommand from core.

11
00:00:34.888 --> 00:00:40.529
ts and registering exactly the same command name — get_clipboard_text — backed by the standard navigator.

12
00:00:40.529 --> 00:00:41.066
clipboard.

13
00:00:41.066 --> 00:00:41.764
readText API.

14
00:00:41.764 --> 00:00:43.000
That's the entire shim.

15
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.330
One wiring step the new file needs: something has to import it so its registerCommand call actually runs.

16
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:51.495
The other shims are pulled in from a central entry point purely for that side effect — add clipboard.

17
00:00:51.495 --> 00:00:52.320
ts alongside secret.

18
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:52.773
ts, opener.

19
00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:55.000
ts and the rest there, or it silently never registers.

20
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:05.000
And the call site, wherever it lives in src/ — a tool, a component, doesn't matter — needs exactly one line, and it's the same line either build reads it in.

21
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.677
Prove it both ways.

22
00:01:05.677 --> 00:01:08.954
npm run tauri dev, trigger the call, confirm it returns whatever's really on your clipboard.

23
00:01:08.954 --> 00:01:14.511
Then, separately, npm run web:dev — no Tauri process behind it at all — trigger the exact same call, and confirm the browser's own clipboard API answers it.

24
00:01:14.511 --> 00:01:19.000
That side-by-side proof is the whole point of the architecture from E68: the call site never knew which one it was talking to.

25
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.729
You've now touched both sides of the seam.

26
00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:26.000
Module 19 turns to process: how the app is actually tested, and how a real change gets contributed and verified before it ships.

